1.1.m:
Formerly, the supreme birth of Brahmā occurred within the golden egg; / and the enveloping of the egg was by water, and of water by fire,

of that [fire] by air, of air by space, and that is enclosed by the origin of elements; / the origin of elements by the cosmic intellect, and the cosmic intellect is enclosed by the unmanifest.

And the emergence of the worlds inside that very egg is described; / the emergence of rivers and mountains is recounted,

and a concise description of the periods of Manus and of cosmic ages; / the description of the dissolution into the tree of Brahman and the creation of creatures by Brahmā;

the succession of cosmic ages, and likewise the establishment of the world; / the reclining of Hari upon the waters, and then the raising up of the earth;

the course of ten births in Keśava on account of the curse of Bhṛgu; / the arrangement of cosmic ages and the division of all life-stages;

and the apportionment of heavenly realms for mortals who travel to heaven; / the origin of beasts and birds is also celebrated;

likewise the etymological explanation, ritual, and the reception of personal Veda-study; / then the secondary creations by Brahmā, which were preceded by intellect, are proclaimed,

and the other three that were not preceded by intellect: thus did he fashion the worlds; / and the origin of Bhṛgu and others from the mouths of Brahmā;

the interval between two cosmic ages is proclaimed, and the conjunction between two creations; / and the description of the creation of offspring by the sages headed by Bhṛgu;

and the status as a Brahmin sage is recounted for the Brahmin sage Vasiṣṭha; / and after that, the commemoration of the Manu Svāyambhuva;

the creation of Nābhi is told, and of the great-souled Rajas; / and the mention of continents, oceans, and mountains;

the division of continents and the inclusion of the oceans among the seven; / those who dwell there are recounted according to the measure of leagues,

and their realms along with their rivers and mountains; / the continents beginning with Jambūdvīpa, surrounded by the seven oceans.

Inside the egg are these worlds, and the earth with seven continents; / the course of the sun and the moon, and likewise of planets and constellations,

and the good and bad fortune of creatures is recounted by the power of the pole star; / the solar chariot was fashioned by Brahmā himself for the sake of purpose.

Arranged by him, the divine sun advances; / the movement of the chariots of the sun and others occurs solely from the pole star.

The heavenly porpoise was conceived, at whose tail the pole star is situated; / and at the end of origin comes dissolution, and at the end of dissolution comes origin.

Regarding the gods, the sages, the Manu, and the host of ancestors, / it is not possible to speak at length; thus it is stated in summary.

Of those past and those to come, together with the Manu Svāyambhuva, / the commemoration of the gods and lords of creatures across the periods of Manus [is made].

Occasional, elemental, and absolute: thus it is remembered; / a threefold dissolution is established for all beings;

the drought caused by the sun, and the terrible cosmic-fire of destruction, / and the clouds that make a single ocean, and likewise the night of the Great-souled One;

the characteristic of the twilight period is indicated, especially that relating to Brahmā; / and the description of the beings and of the seven worlds.

And here the Raurava and other hells of sinners are recounted by me; / and the definitive determination of the transformation of all living entities;

the secondary creation of Brahmā, and the description of the universal dissolution; / and in each cosmic age, the destruction even of great beings.

Having reflected well and realized the impermanence even of Brahmā, / the pernicious nature of sensual pleasures, and the hardship of cyclic existence,

and the rarity of liberation, and seeing faults through detachment; / having abandoned the manifest and unmanifest, being established in Brahman,

he is tranquil through seeing diversity, and from that he turns toward That; / then, having transcended the threefold suffering, devoid of form, untainted,

having attained the bliss of Brahman, he fears nothing whatsoever. / Thus the summary of contents has been described according to the standard [authority],

wherein the transformations of creation and dissolution of the universe are celebrated, / and also the worldly engagement of beings and the fruits of renunciation;

the appearance of Vasiṣṭha, and likewise the birth of Śakti; / and his destruction by Saudāsa, caused by Viśvāmitra.

And how the lord Parāśara was born to Adṛśyantī, / and how the sage Vyāsa was born to the daughter of the Pitṛs;

and how the wise son Śuka was born; / and how the enmity toward Viśvāmitra was caused by Parāśara;

and the fire gathered by Vasiṣṭha with the desire to slay Viśvāmitra, / which was absorbed by the wise lord Kaṇva for the sake of reconciliation;

by the divine one wishing well to Viśvāmitra, O brahmins, [the well-wisher] of brahmins; / and how the lord, the revered Vyāsa, divided

the one four-quartered Veda again into four out of grace for all, / and the division into branches was made again by his disciples and grand-disciples;

and another sixty-seven millions, O wise one, / and twenty thousand—this is the time without the addition;

this is the calculation of a manvantara here in human years. / Fourteen times this time is remembered as a day of Brahmā.

At the end of it comes the dissolution called the cosmic dissolution of Brahmā; / then all the three worlds, earth, the atmosphere, and the rest, are burned up.

Afflicted by the heat, the residents of Maharloka go to Janaloka. / But when the three worlds have become a single ocean, Brahmā, the best of the knowers of Brahman,

lying on the serpent bed, sleeps, augmented by the devouring of the three worlds, / the god, all-pervading lord of the world, contemplated by yogins residing in Janaloka.

For that night is of equal duration, and at its end he creates again. / Such is a year of Brahmā, and such are his hundred years;

a hundred of his years is the full lifespan of that great soul. / One parārdha of this Brahmā has passed, O sinless one;

at its end was the great kalpa renowned as the Pādma. / Of the second parārdha, which is now current, O king,

this kalpa, considered the first, is called Vārāha. / How that lord, Brahmā named Nārāyaṇa, at the beginning of the kalpa,

the reason why your husband was not invited here today: / he holds a skull-bowl, wears skins, and his body is covered in ashes;

bearing a trident, shaven-headed, and naked, he always delights in the cremation ground; / and he constantly smears all his limbs with funeral ash;

he wears a tiger skin and has an elephant hide for his covering; / a garland of skulls is on his head, and a khaṭvāṅga club rests in his hand;

having bound a viper around his waist, and likewise a bracelet of bones on his arm, / and having made Vāsuki, king of serpents, his sacred thread,

he wanders constantly on the earth in this form. / Naked attendants, piśācas, and manifold hosts of spirits [surround him];

three-eyed and trident-bearing, he is ever intent on song and dance; / such and other contemptible things your husband constantly does.

He would cause me shame; how could he be in the presence of the gods? / What sort of clothing is his? He is not suited for an assembly.

Because of these faults, child, and out of shame before the worlds, / no invitation was extended to him by me for this reason, O daughter.

At the conclusion of this sacrifice, having performed worship together with you, / I shall bring your husband, the three-eyed one, along with you,
